this morning on the dish chef Anthony  Sasso he was born in the Hudson River  Valley of New York to a family with  roots in Spain after culinary school in  New York City and jobs at some of the  city's top restaurants he travelled to  Spain to master its secrets he brought  that knowledge back to New York's Casa  mano where he earned five consecutive  Michelin stars this year he became  executive chef at tapas bar at La Serena  in Chelsea's maritime hotel the main  restaurant is all Italian a project of  Mario Batali and Joseph basi homage but  the bar is all his featuring an entirely  separate menu of small Spanish dishes  perfect I might say with drinks chef  Anthony Sasso good morning welcome to  the dish good morning  I think we're looking at half of that  menu right now some of what you can tell  us that's here well the big attraction  there's the pyah pyah is one thing that  we can do at Serena that we weren't able  to Casa moto  the restaurant is probably ten times the  size the kitchen is you know 20 times  the size of the old Russia that I came  from but that's definitely one of the  anchors of Spanish cuisine and the fun  thing about it in Serina is we're  serving this alongside in a different  room right next to some of the best  pastas in the city so it's a really fun  concept that we started about six months  ago and it's really taken off you know a  lot of the chef's we've had here grow up  and they've got a family that's inspired  them and so many ways to cook and you  sort of said that it was you know trial  and error on your own early on yeah I  mean I always had fun in the kitchen and  playing around my father's a carpenter  so he was really good with his hands and  building and my mom always had a way to  kind of like work two jobs or three jobs  just to kind of like keep everything  else together so me and my three sisters  we were stuck in a kitchen and kind of  like had to fend for ourselves a little  bit as far as like making dinner we had  grandmother's nose clear close by that  always kind of like had something on the  stove that we would either pitch in and  eat or help them kind of like wrap up  together for dinner time so whatever the  lightbulb go offer you that this was  what you wanted to do I think it was it  was right about that time when the Food  Network was really taking off so you  were able to see these chefs in their  kitchens put together their job outside  the restaurant and I got really into it  I lot of my first jobs were in kitchens  and restaurants  and I loved it I just always write  really write names I mean Bobby Flay  Wylie Dufresne Mario Batali right so as  a young cook that's kind of like what  you're going for you going for the best  in the in the business and then I  mentioned you went to Spain to learn as  well what was that experience like for  you well I wouldn't Spain the first few  times I went there was just to kind of  like travel and to see my family so my  first day there one of these trips my  uncle brought me said his favorite  restaurant and you know I had bought a  one-way ticket that trip and didn't know  if I was ever going to come back home  and the second day I walked in the  kitchen and met the chef and the third  day I had a job and I said I ended up  staying for a full year that time the  tapas which everybody knows from Spain  now a part of laughs arena that's not a  common thing it's like a restaurant  within a restaurant how did that concept  even come across so I had kind I had  been customer for a very long time it  was kind of like this little place that  I won't say was containing B but I think  that's the way like Mario Batali looked  at he was kind of like how do we give  him a larger platform right we made you  an offer you couldn't refuse okay and  the way he made it was kind of Godfather  asked where we two of us met alone not a  horse had involved was there maybe a  pig's head we met at his restaurant odo  kind of in the back of this empty room  it was nine o'clock in the morning we  were the only two there so he kind of  said I want to do something with this  huge space that I have that's kind of we  should be used for multiple purposes so  he asked me if I wanted to come over I  offer tapas food in his Italian  restaurant in the bar area so did you  say yes right away  no so I had Mona's very endearing to me  I've been there for a long time so he  basically gave me a night to think about  it he was like I'll just give me an  answer tomorrow morning he called me  back four hours later it was like so  what are you gonna do and I mean at that  point I was just like yes I'm on board I  think it's a great idea there's a great  marriage we are on the dish so I will  ask you to sign our dish I'm not used to  plates these large let me ask you this  as well if you could have a meal anyone  past or present who would it be my  grandmother Nonya she passed away not  that long ago it was her food that kind  of got me into cooking and I still think  about what she would put on a play  whenever I'm thinking of putting  something on the plate myself so you see  it in the cuisine as well a little love  in there thank you so much yes chef  Anthony Sasso for more on him and the  dish you can head to our website at CBS  this morning dot-com
 

